WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.328 --> 00:00:02.911
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.810
- Well, welcome to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.360
It's my pleasure to be able
to host these conversations,

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.340
and to really continue
to talk about topics

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.170
that I think are essential
and important for us

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.650
as a society.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.260
And so today I'm, again honored

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.650
to be able to welcome two guests

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.860
from different walks of life,

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.400
who are gonna be able to share
their unique experiences,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.950
but also share some parallels

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.410
and overlapping themes as well.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.990
The topic for today's conversation

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.250
will be where do we go from here.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.630
And I think in a lot of ways
this is a highly relevant topic

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.180
and conversation.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.500
I mean, to be completely honest,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.940
it seems like we're just
going from one tragic event

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.690
to the next on a weekly basis,
sometimes on a daily basis.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:44.920
And so I think it's
really important for us

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:45.870
to be able to sit back

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.670
and process how we
actually move through that.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.410
So I'm honored to be able
to host today's two guests.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.200
The first is Dr. Amy Arnsten.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.920
And Amy Arnsten is the
Albert E. Kent Professor

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.260
of psychology and neuroscience at Yale.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.620
She directs a research
lab where she looks at

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.810
and researches the processes
and circuits in the brain

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:09.120
that are affected by stress and by aging,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.020
and how those can lead
to cognitive deficits.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.220
She's made a whole host
of different research,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.460
seminal research contributions
to the field over the years.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.120
She's also a member of the prestigious

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.010
National Academy of Medicine.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.640
And so we're thrilled to
have Amy here with us.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.980
One thing I will say, one of
the things I really appreciate

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.560
in addition to all the accolades

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.710
is how Amy leads with passion.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.810
This is something that you
can see in the classroom

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.830
when she's teaching, in the research lab

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.970
and then how she engages in society.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.550
So Amy, welcome to the Addy Hour,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.130
definitely grateful to have
you here with us today.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.830
- Thank you so much Nii, a joy to be here.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.200
- Our second guest is
none other than Lecrae.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.190
Lecrae is a multi Grammy award
winning (indistinct) artist,

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:54.570
who has evolved into a New
York Times bestselling author,

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.650
entrepreneur, speaker, thought
leader, and philanthropist,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.130
all while co owning

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.520
and serving as president of Reach Records.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.400
So he's someone who's had a lot of impact

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.980
in a lot of different spheres.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.840
He's also sold millions of
copies of his music worldwide.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.520
He has won multiple Grammy
Awards, Dove Awards,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.750
a Soul Train Music Award

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.290
and also had a BET hip hop nomination.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.170
But again, addition to all the accolades,

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.890
one thing I really appreciate about him

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.810
is how invested he is in community

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.010
and how he has continued
to make those investments

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.530
in several different ways.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.140
One of those more recent
has been all the initiatives

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.680
that he's taken part in

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.270
and really trying to rebuild
the West side of Metro Atlanta.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.660
In addition to other things that he's done

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.960
in the Atlanta area and worldwide.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.300
So really grateful to have
Lecrae here on the program

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.590
with us today as well,
thanks for being here.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.683
- Honored, honored, sincerely.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.450
- So we're ready to jump
into this conversation

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.030
and excited to really think
deeply about this aspect

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.130
of where do we try and go from here.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.470
For any of you who are regular listeners,

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.760
one of the things I like
to do is really to check in

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.870
with our guests first, just
to see how you're doing

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.080
in the midst of everything
that's going on.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.140
Clearly this has been
another tragic week for us

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.110
as a society with ongoing
killings of black men and boys

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.540
at the hands of police.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.820
And it's heavy, I mean, that's
a honestly heavy place to be.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.780
I mean, I can say personally,
a lot of times I moved through

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.400
a lot of different emotions
when those things come up.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.760
There's a feeling
sometimes of personal fear,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.000
just being completely honest.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.490
There's feelings of frustration, anger,

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:26.580
there's feelings of
exhaustion and tiredness.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.010
And sometimes I know I even shift

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.710
towards being a little bit numb

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.710
to just seeing this kind of
happening over and over again.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.850
And then at the same time I
know that I have a deep need

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.140
and desire for healing and hope.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.427
So it's really a mixture of things

00:03:38.427 --> 00:03:40.660
and it kind of moves depending on the day

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.000
and where I'm at any
specific point in time.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.470
But I thought it would be
really good for all of us,

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.940
the three of us here and for our listeners

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.920
to really at least start
out talking through

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.430
how you're dealing with
everything that's going on

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.190
in society and even in
your personal lives.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:55.910
So Amy, if we could go
ahead and start with you

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.690
if you'd be willing to share.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.810
- Sure, and I also feeling weary

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:05.420
from such a continued stress.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.630
Much of the past year for me personally,

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:11.470
I was so worried about my mother who's 99,

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.990
which is wonderful, but
she was in an assisted

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.250
living facility and it was a
real race between the virus

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:22.033
and the vaccine and thank
God she got the vaccine.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.790
And it was the same
thing with the election,

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:30.090
where it was just this
overwhelming anxiety.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.890
And thankfully Biden won.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:37.057
But at this point when I have
this very childish feeling of,

00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:39.810
"Oh, can't it all be all better now."

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:44.510
And instead dealing now
with the real problems

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.100
that are still very much there.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.580
So for example, my mother has gone blind

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.170
and how to help her.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:53.803
And then at a national level,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.640
today as we talk just the national anxiety

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.413
about what's gonna happen
in the Chauvin trial.

00:05:03.907 --> 00:05:08.907
And I think over the past four
years, so much got exposed

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:13.250
about the many weaknesses in America

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.300
that we had so hoped
had really been solved

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.320
a lot more than it has.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.150
And this realization, we
have a long way to go.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:24.983
And

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:31.060
that's going to take a lot of help.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.710
We have to really take care
of ourselves and each other

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.430
if we're going to make progress.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.760
And to relate to today's
conversation, I find for myself

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.860
that understanding how the
brain responds to stress

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:48.820
has really helped me have
compassion for myself

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:50.520
when I'm feeling inadequate

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.700
and not living up to what I hope to be.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.340
And I think it's wonderful
to have this opportunity

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.600
to share this here today,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.410
'cause I think compassion and forgiveness,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:04.490
whether they come from
science or religion or art,

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.640
helps us to heal and can give
us that strength we need.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.610
- That's so well said.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.730
Thank you for sharing, so honestly about,

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.080
even the personal challenges and what,

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.640
the places that you find hope and healing

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.090
in the midst of this as well.

00:06:18.334 --> 00:06:19.167
Lecrae, what about you?

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:21.603
How are you managing
at this current moment?

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:26.323
- Well, it's interesting.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:33.170
I think, 2016 is when I
really, 2014 through 16,

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:40.610
is when I really found
myself hyper-focused

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:44.060
on a lot of the racial tensions

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.950
and political tensions in America.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:50.600
And I did not consider how it
would personally affect me.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.300
I think I just went in blind
and not recognizing the real,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.060
I didn't understand the
science of stress or anxiety

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.340
or depression, any of those things.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:05.340
And I just stuffed all of
those emotions and feelings

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.130
deep down inside

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:13.130
which ultimately resulted
in a mental breakdown.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.900
And I think from 2018 until now,

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:23.260
I'm so much more aware of

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:30.380
what hyper-focusing on all
of those particular things

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:35.380
can do to me, and so I
think in this season of life

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:40.190
I'm working to process solutions,

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:45.120
I'm working to process how we can navigate

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.120
and positive things that we
can do in light of it all.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.890
Because I think you can become
a nihilist in a lot of ways

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:58.280
if you just look at how
crazy everything is.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:03.280
So for me, I've been trying to be helpful

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.440
in as many ways as possible.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.173
I've been trying to figure
out how do we invest

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:14.160
in potential Dante Wrights,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.950
invest in the younger
generation of George Floyds

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.580
to create awareness and insight

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.370
so that they can find themselves

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.170
in a healthier place in life.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:32.437
And then at the same time thinking through

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:41.470
what it looks like to communicate
empathy to law enforcement

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.010
and people on the other side of the aisle.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:49.010
And so all that to say,
to get super scientific

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.740
for some people, I am
believing in neuro-plasticity,

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.750
I am trying to focus on new things

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:01.165
so that changes can happen and
healing can occur in my mind.

00:09:01.165 --> 00:09:02.100
(All laughing)

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.900
- Well said, well, I was
joking before we got on there,

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.850
we're gonna flip the roles a little bit,

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.620
have Lecrae expand on the neuroscience

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.803
and have Amy talk about
her experiences with music.

00:09:11.686 --> 00:09:12.519
(All laughing)

00:09:12.519 --> 00:09:14.790
And there's room for that,
there's room for that.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:16.430
But I appreciate you sharing a little bit

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.980
of the journey as well, and
both of what you've shared,

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:20.500
both from personal experiences

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.120
and what you've tried to do.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.760
Lecrae I'd be curious to
hear you expand on that,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.087
even a little bit more on what you,

00:09:25.087 --> 00:09:27.880
'cause you talked about how
you've gotten to this place.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.080
What have you seen be
effective in your journey?

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.110
And on the flip side, things
that you would also say

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.640
that have been ineffective,
that you would have avoided

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.320
or that you would do
differently going forward.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.320
- Yeah, I think one is
understanding your limitations.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.610
There are certain things
that you can know about

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:50.900
but you may not need to see.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.763
And I think in the age we're in,

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:59.950
I'm grateful for the
accountability of video

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:02.390
but it doesn't mean I need
to watch all the videos.

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.730
It doesn't mean that's healthy for me

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:09.073
to see people being murdered via video.

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:14.990
And so part of it for me is not
inundating myself with that,

00:10:17.722 --> 00:10:22.722
with those visuals, figuring
out, okay, what am I doing?

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:27.070
What kinds of things can
I do that make me feel

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.910
like I'm investing and
I'm working toward change?

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:32.660
I think that's been helpful for me.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:39.610
And then more or less is
in light of everything

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.478
going on societally, is
I've had to find myself,

00:10:44.478 --> 00:10:46.290
where does my hope lie?

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:48.130
And what am I grateful for?

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:51.830
And how can I meditate on
those particular things

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:52.823
more often?

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.190
The things that I wish I
would have done different is

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:03.190
I think that I found
myself very hyper-focused

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:08.480
on all of the negativity, and
I did not look historically

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.490
at the change that had occurred

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:13.680
and that what it may take
to see more change occur.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.680
I just looked at kind of the
pessimistic side of everything,

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:24.180
and I just found myself very low and very,

00:11:25.653 --> 00:11:27.231
it was a dark time.

00:11:27.231 --> 00:11:30.790
And if I could go back, I
think I would have learned

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.690
that hyper-focusing on all of
that was not healthy for me.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.229
- Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:11:36.229 --> 00:11:37.620
And I think that's something
we'll probably come back to

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:40.440
in this conversation
to, even thinking about

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.560
how we get into that hyperfocused state

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:43.480
and what's happening in our brain,

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:46.230
that allows that to occur
and ways to even manage that.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:48.760
I think it's also great that
you brought the idea of hope

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:49.700
and healing as well.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:51.830
And Amy, you were hinting
at that and mentioning that

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:53.870
when you talked about your perspective

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.360
with neuroscience as well.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.040
So I'd be curious if you
could share for our listeners

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:59.120
how you got into this journey

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:00.500
or your journey to get to this place

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.630
of thinking about the neuroscience
of stress and resilience.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:06.040
And then also how that
helps you even more so

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:08.280
with the hope that you were talking about.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:12.030
- Sure, I actually began wondering

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:15.243
about neuroscience and
stress when I was a teenager.

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:19.080
We had a lot of illness
and death in my family,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.930
with loved ones when I was that age.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.770
I watched as like my
parents, people around me,

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:33.770
who you rely on as a kid and
suddenly they're this swirl.

00:12:35.363 --> 00:12:39.250
There's a quick sand there,
you can't rely on them anymore

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:41.203
'cause they weren't thinking straight.

00:12:43.452 --> 00:12:46.650
I actually began volunteering

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:49.590
at the State Psychiatric
Hospital in New Jersey

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.940
to learn more about mental illness.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:55.290
And it shows how old I
am that this was a time

00:12:55.290 --> 00:13:00.290
when there were these huge
hospitals, 2,000 patients.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.460
And you can argue whether that was better

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:06.003
than having people out
on the street, homeless.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:11.080
But volunteering there I got to know

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.000
several patients quite well.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:16.530
And one gentleman had schizophrenia

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:21.530
and had been an astrophysicist,
and we were able to talk

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:25.300
about astronomy in this great way,

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:29.170
totally logical, you'd
never know he was ill.

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.370
And then one day, while we're doing that

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.690
someone mentioned the name
of this really cruel doctor.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.910
And just hearing that man's name

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:44.680
this gentleman just instantly dissolved

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.180
into what's called word
salad, thought disorder,

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:52.250
one clanging phrase after another

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:54.970
that really didn't make sense.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:59.770
And then as he calmed down,
his thoughts (indistinct)

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:03.150
and we continued our talk
about the solar system.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:07.623
And it was like this
explosive seed crystal to me.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:11.780
This shows I have to understand

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.530
what stress does to the brain,

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:18.460
because it's such a clue
as to what's underlying

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:21.500
so many disorders,
including schizophrenia.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.910
And so I've really studied it ever since.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:29.289
And thankfully we've been able
to make a lot of progress,

00:14:29.289 --> 00:14:33.293
and what we've learned, we,
meaning the field as well as me,

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:36.510
that when you have a stress

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:40.600
that makes you feel out of
control, that's a key part of it,

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:45.050
if you feel up to it, these
toxic changes don't happen.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:47.870
But if you feel threatened
or out of control,

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.600
you get this flood of chemicals

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:55.300
that takes what's called the
prefrontal cortex offline.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:59.960
And the prefrontal cortex is
one of our most newly evolved

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:04.110
parts of our brain that
subserves abstract reasoning,

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:05.623
top-down control.

00:15:07.011 --> 00:15:09.360
So you really need it
when you're going through

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:12.900
a complex stressor in particular.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:16.360
And at the same time this
flood of stress chemicals

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.850
strengthens our primitive circuits

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:21.770
that make us unconsciously reacts,

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.770
our emotional reactions and habits.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:26.480
So it's this double whammy

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:30.480
where you're losing your
rational cheerleader

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:32.460
and at the same time strengthening

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:34.670
these primitive reactions.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:38.650
And your brain can really get in a rut

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:43.220
where it develops a vicious
cycle and gets stuck there.

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.630
And so a large part of what
my lab is trying to do,

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:51.630
is understand what is it that
makes prefrontal cortex weak

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:55.270
versus stronger, so we
can develop medication.

00:15:55.270 --> 00:16:00.000
So if you're in a rut
you can get some help

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.730
on getting to a healthier place.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:06.730
So it's been a long journey
cause I'm an old woman,

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:10.407
but I can really place it back (chuckles)

00:16:10.407 --> 00:16:14.650
to those childhood experiences.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:15.690
- Yeah, that's really powerful,

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:17.310
I mean, to know that
that initial experience

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:20.170
had such a long-term impact on you,

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.320
in your trajectory, in your passion,

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.550
I think that that says a
lot in the fact that you're,

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:28.020
I mean, it sounds like you're
motivated by the people

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.310
that you're serving in
a lot of ways as well.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:32.230
- Yeah, very much,

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:36.120
and sometimes that person's me. (chuckles)

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.300
- So what, I'd be curious to
hear even more about that.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.850
How does that part come into
play, the person being you?

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:45.070
Because I know it's attention
and sometimes there are ways

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:48.440
that different of us as
scientists kind of reconcile that

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:50.440
in different ways, so
it'd be great to hear,

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.150
how you've done that over the years.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:57.143
- Well, some of it is when
I find myself losing it.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:01.410
Lecrae was saying, finding your limits.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.457
And I've also really come to say,

00:17:04.457 --> 00:17:09.010
"Okay, I can feel that
my brain is not going

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:13.163
into a good place, stop watching
the news for hours on end.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.123
Let's go for a walk in the woods that,

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.807
knowing what your own limits are

00:17:20.807 --> 00:17:23.400
and what makes you feel better,

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:26.750
which is individual for
everybody, but learning that.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:30.120
And so you can take
care of yourself better.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.550
And I'm also taking one of the medications

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:35.870
my lab discovered, but
that's cause I'm an old lady

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:38.282
and not just as a stressed person.

00:17:38.282 --> 00:17:40.580
(all laughing)

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:43.380
- Yeah, well I mean, I
think that's powerful

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:45.930
in a lot of ways, and I
appreciate your honesty

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:47.810
in sharing that as well.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:48.900
- Who can say that?

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.690
Who say, "I'm taking a
medication my lab discovered."

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:57.690
That's like, I have no
comprehension of this, is amazing.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.790
- It is, it is, it's a good point.

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:02.930
- We don't know if it's working though.

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:05.570
(laughing loudly)

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:07.200
- We can, we can ask those around you.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.653
I have a feeling they would
say it's working. (chuckles)

00:18:11.495 --> 00:18:13.320
Lecrae, how do you, I
mean, you react to it,

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.520
but how do you react to even components

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:16.370
that Amy's talking about

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:18.380
in terms of how the brain's working?

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.060
Like does that, hearing that
now and thinking backwards

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:21.893
to what you've already talked about

00:18:21.893 --> 00:18:24.700
does that give you even a way to interpret

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:26.440
some of the things that you were saying?

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:31.393
- Yeah, I mean, it's just, I'm fascinated,

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.350
I think like organizing chaos

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.900
is something that's
very fascinating for me,

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:43.900
and I think the idea
that I walk through life

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:50.640
and all the chaos that just ensues

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:56.400
and I didn't have categories
for what was happening.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.060
I didn't have language for it like,

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:02.020
"Oh, this is stress I'm
feeling," you know what I mean?

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:04.570
I don't think I had a context for it.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:07.380
And then even to go steps
deeper and talk about,

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:10.180
well my prefrontal cortex
isn't working properly

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:13.773
and all those things are very helpful.

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.010
Some things are just obvious to us.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:22.050
I really, I'm grateful for the

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:26.900
kind of playing field being leveled

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.603
as it pertains to mental health,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:33.420
more so because I don't think people think

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:36.230
it's as scientific as it is.

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:41.230
I think they find themselves
connecting their brain

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:48.070
to their essence, like your
identity in a lot of ways.

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:54.420
And your biology is not the
full extent of your identity.

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:57.160
Sometimes, it's kinda of like right now,

00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:00.290
like people don't know I'm
wearing contact lenses.

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:03.160
But my identity is not that I can't,

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:05.220
I don't think of like, "Oh
my gosh, I need contact,

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:07.750
so I'm a terrible person,"
you know what I mean?

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:08.850
I don't think like that.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:13.850
I think how can I fix this
blindness that I suffer from?

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:15.620
Do I wear some glasses?

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:17.240
Do I put in some contacts?

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:18.390
And then I can move forward.

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:23.010
But it's not like a crushing
weight on my worth and my value

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:27.930
but oftentimes my brain not
functioning as it should,

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:32.930
like a disorder you tend
to say, "I'm terrible."

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:34.960
You know what I mean like,

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:38.547
I didn't know, like I have
ADD, I don't know if it's ADHD,

00:20:38.547 --> 00:20:43.547
but it definitely ADD, and
I didn't know that as a kid,

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:47.250
I just thought I was as
impulsive, mischievous,

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:50.467
irrational person, and
now as an adult it's like,

00:20:50.467 --> 00:20:54.180
"Oh, I wish I would've known that."

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:58.750
And so I think it's very
insightful and it's comforting

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:03.493
to hear Amy talk about these
things, it gives me comfort.

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:06.060
- Yeah, that's really well said

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:10.153
- I'm so glad, 'cause
it does that for me too.

00:21:10.153 --> 00:21:11.430
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.770
I think that's a place
that we all need to be

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:14.603
in a lot of the ways.

00:21:14.603 --> 00:21:15.820
I mean, even the point that you brought up

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:18.390
about leveling the playing field.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:21.240
Not to be too pejorative
to those as academics,

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.940
but we have this tendency
to get stuck in our bubble.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.530
You don't talk to
anybody about the science

00:21:25.530 --> 00:21:26.800
outside of that bubble.

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:29.350
And then we don't even
interpret it for our own selves.

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:30.860
So, I mean, Amy I think
everything you're talking about,

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:33.430
I mean, I see that as
unique in a lot of ways

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:36.320
as reflective as you've
been about your work

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:39.350
and your own emotions as
you're walking through that

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:41.810
and being able to, I mean,
the fancy word people use

00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:44.920
is metacognition, to be able
to think about your thinking

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.890
and what you're going
through while it's happening.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:49.320
And to be able to have those tools,

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:51.230
I think it makes it even
that much more powerful

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:53.880
to talk about your experience
so that those outside

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:55.760
of the scientific realm can learn from it

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.810
and actually apply it and
see that we're applying it

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:01.023
in our own lives as well, I
mean, I think that's huge.

00:22:01.970 --> 00:22:04.430
- Well, and just a reminder that often

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:06.430
you can't do that in the moment,

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:09.810
'cause stress is taking
exactly those circuits offline.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:13.680
But then not being mad at
yourself about that and going back

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:16.450
and revisiting and having that.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:17.713
- Yeah, yeah, so true.

00:22:17.713 --> 00:22:21.757
- I don't think people sit
back and tell themselves like,

00:22:21.757 --> 00:22:25.300
"Oh man, my prefrontal
cortex is short-circuiting

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:27.477
right now," like, yeah-

00:22:27.477 --> 00:22:28.310
- I do.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:31.143
(laughing loudly)

00:22:33.117 --> 00:22:35.680
- So, I mean, on that note
that's actually a great segue

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:37.920
'cause I'm curious, we started this,

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:39.010
thinking about where do we go from here?

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:40.820
How do we take all these principles

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:43.980
that you all are talking
about and apply them right now

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:45.570
in our current situation?

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:47.620
Maybe not in the moment as we're seeing

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.800
and another unjust killing occurring,

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:52.450
but in this moment as we're conversing

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:55.390
and thinking about how we're
going to deal with that,

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:57.173
if and when it happens again.

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:02.483
Amy any thoughts you wanna
jump in with to start off?

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.940
- I wanna go back to what we just said

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:11.830
to add one more thought
that I think it's helpful

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:16.830
to think of mental health
as a continuous spectrum.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:23.130
That there's this continuum
between health and illness,

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:27.940
and also that a strength in one context

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:31.450
can be a weakness in
another, including with ADD,

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:34.840
there's times when having
that kind of energy

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:38.990
and being distracted by
something that's just occurred

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:42.020
can be life-saving for
a person or a community

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:43.610
who's with that person.

00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:48.610
So I think that helps reduce
stigma and, and gives us,

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:53.550
it's both more realistic, but
also more compassionate view

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:58.550
about brain health and the recognition

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:04.180
that if you've had either genetic insults,

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:09.230
a physical insult, an
emotional or physical trauma

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.423
that those are times when
you need extra self-care,

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:17.350
recognizing limits that can shift

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:21.280
and one can be more fragile
as Lecrae was talking about.

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:25.840
So really taking care
of our minds, brains,

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.280
the way many people take
care of their bodies

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:29.350
with working out.

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:32.730
And I think some people are
already trying to do that

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:34.693
with like drinking caffeine,

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:39.460
but doing it in a much
broader way with all the needs

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:44.460
and that can help give
us more effective view

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:47.043
on mental health.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:53.280
For what you were just saying this idea of

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:58.170
how do we help ourselves
feel more in control

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:04.160
and give ourselves what we
need to be able to help ourself

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:12.060
be our better self again,
and it's so individual.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:16.300
So it's, again you have to
really know who you are,

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:18.130
what are your triggers?

00:25:18.130 --> 00:25:22.010
What are the things that
help to make you feel better

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.850
and important that they're the things

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:27.660
that don't make you feel
worse in the morning.

00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:32.660
So alcohol, drugs can make
you temporarily feel better

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:34.533
but then you're worst.

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:39.950
What are the things that
give you sustained goodness?

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:43.490
Even if it isn't so dramatic instantly,

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:46.970
it's really there for
you for the long run.

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:51.020
And I think there's many things,

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:55.490
for some people it's things
like meditation and exercise.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:58.390
Oh, and that reminds me,

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:01.790
I got a call once from Arianna Huffington,

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:05.700
who was writing a piece on
the civil rights leaders

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:10.700
of the 20th century and how
they had to do self care

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:15.300
to get themselves through the long haul.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:18.330
And how like Rosa Parks did yoga.

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:21.850
And so they really recognize
something right from the start,

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:26.610
this is gonna be a very,
very long and hard mission.

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:29.890
We have to take care of ourselves.

00:26:29.890 --> 00:26:32.700
And I think we have forgotten that

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:35.930
and are having to rediscover it.

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:40.223
And so the kinds of things
that can help people,

00:26:42.650 --> 00:26:47.650
feeling gratitude, gaining
perspective from that,

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:49.830
from walking through the woods

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:51.953
is one of the things I like to do,

00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:57.790
through community, through
artistic expression

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:01.250
and music in particular, I think can be

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:05.420
particularly effective 'cause
music is so much about emotion

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:10.420
both expressing it and
receiving it by listening to it.

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:13.150
And my son is also a musician.

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:18.150
So I get to watch this closeup
and I get to see the comments

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:22.450
on his YouTube videos,
just like with Lecrae's,

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:27.340
where people are saying, "I
have just lost this loved one

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:30.270
and your music has made
all the difference to me

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:32.717
giving me hope again."

00:27:33.630 --> 00:27:37.103
Someone who is suicidal
and listening to the music,

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:41.640
gave them what they
needed to keep going on.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:46.640
So I think blessings
rhyming with stressings

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:50.555
is really important Lecrae,
so thank you for that.

00:27:50.555 --> 00:27:51.388
(laughing loudly)

00:27:51.388 --> 00:27:52.891
- Well said.

00:27:52.891 --> 00:27:54.750
I didn't know you were gonna
bring out some lyricism.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:56.160
I'm very impressed.
(Amy laughing)

00:27:56.160 --> 00:27:57.380
Very impressed.

00:27:57.380 --> 00:27:58.743
- Bravo, bravo.

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:03.930
That's great, I would wholeheartedly agree

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:07.320
with so much of what she said.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:12.320
I think, I look at my own
life in light of everything,

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.660
I was, I experienced a
lot of historic trauma.

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:18.513
I was abused.

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:22.193
I experienced neglect.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:25.890
The men I looked up to

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:28.690
I was oftentimes visiting them in prison.

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:32.580
And so I didn't realize those
are all contributing factors

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:37.440
to my development and my mental health.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:42.440
And so I look at it
like, the average person,

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:47.303
if you think about their stress
level as like a paper towel,

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:53.680
when you put, if you've got
a paper towel on top of a cup

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:56.780
or something like that, and
you put some, I don't know,

00:28:56.780 --> 00:29:01.780
some rice on top of that paper
towel, it's gonna sink down,

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:04.690
but if there's like a
rubber band around it,

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:07.270
it'll sustain and hold the rice.

00:29:07.270 --> 00:29:09.570
Well, I look at my historical trauma,

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:13.750
as like drips of water on my paper towel.

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:17.210
And so when someone puts the rice on it,

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:18.530
I'm more vulnerable.

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:21.760
I'm more vulnerable to having a breakdown,

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.690
to having that stress snap.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:29.690
And I think that's what's
currently been helpful for me.

00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:34.110
I didn't know this in 2014
through 2016, I didn't know that.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:36.450
So I'm engaging these
stressful circumstances

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:40.030
not realizing that I'm dealing
with a wet paper towel.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:45.030
And now, it's like I know, I
have a sensitive brain, right?

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:50.580
Like my brain is sensitive,
it's hypersensitive.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:55.280
I get false alarms in terms
of anxiety all the time.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:59.297
And so I have to,

00:29:59.297 --> 00:30:04.090
and knowing that I can
think more rationally,

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:05.173
and I probably have to spend more time,

00:30:05.173 --> 00:30:08.350
like she was just talking
about Rosa Parks doing yoga,

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:11.090
I spend more time meditating on truth.

00:30:11.090 --> 00:30:14.140
I think if you,

00:30:14.140 --> 00:30:17.070
they say that people who
know counterfeit money

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:20.170
they don't study counterfeit money,

00:30:20.170 --> 00:30:22.520
they study real money so well,

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.960
that when they see counterfeit,

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:27.280
they can find, they can
pick it out immediately.

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:29.980
And that's kinda what I
find myself having to do.

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:34.730
I have to spend time
meditating on what is true

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:39.280
and what is good, and
not irrational things,

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:43.340
so that when this false alarm
gets triggered in my brain,

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:45.910
I can say, that's not what this is.

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:48.290
That's not what's happening right now.

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:53.290
And so, someone gets murdered
on national television,

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:59.730
I think it's stressful to anyone, right?

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:02.830
But for me, I can,

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:06.510
without having meditated on what's true,

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:10.380
I can get very irrational
and think this is the end.

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.093
it's gonna happen for me, this is,

00:31:12.093 --> 00:31:13.700
I don't know, my kids are,

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:17.020
and I can just start spinning
out of control irrationally,

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:21.360
which makes chaos happen, and
so I have to meditate and say,

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:24.190
hey, listen, what's true about this?

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:28.480
Okay, can you absolutely prove
that someone's gonna kick in

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:30.630
your door tonight and kill your kids?

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:33.020
No, I can't, so let's not go there.

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:33.853
You know what I mean?

00:31:33.853 --> 00:31:37.580
Let's not get too wound up
in that particular place.

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:39.940
And I found that that's
been very helpful for me,

00:31:39.940 --> 00:31:44.180
is really meditating on what
I know is true to fight back

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.410
that hypersensitivity
going on in my brain.

00:31:47.410 --> 00:31:48.957
- That's so good, and that
ties in it makes me think

00:31:48.957 --> 00:31:51.360
of one of the points that
you made earlier too,

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.280
you're not to get too
far outside the scope

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.850
but you talked about
both sides of the aisle,

00:31:55.850 --> 00:31:57.930
for us as black men in this country

00:31:57.930 --> 00:32:00.210
but then also for those
who are in law enforcement.

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:01.680
'Cause I have friends
who are police officers

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:04.380
and if you've listened
to some of the commentary

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:06.570
with what happened to the individual

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:08.980
that was in the armed
services that got pulled over.

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:11.350
People have been talking
about the mental state

00:32:11.350 --> 00:32:13.800
or the hypervigilant state
that the police were in

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:15.730
when they pulled that person over.

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:19.109
What was going through
their minds at that moment?

00:32:19.109 --> 00:32:19.942
- Yes.

00:32:19.942 --> 00:32:23.137
- And we also have to talk
about those situations too

00:32:23.137 --> 00:32:24.800
and those individuals where they're coming

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:25.960
into that situation from.

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:26.793
- Yes.

00:32:26.793 --> 00:32:29.080
- So I know, I mean, being
on the receiving end of that,

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:31.690
being pulled over, I was
coming back from a talk,

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:33.120
at Emory of all places

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:34.990
got back at one o'clock in the morning.

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:36.720
On the way home, lived in the part of town

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:38.010
that was kind of off the beaten path

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:40.010
and got pulled over at
one o'clock the morning

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:42.530
for no reason, so everything
that's running through my mind,

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:44.980
I was definitely in a hypervigilant state.

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:48.070
But then also thinking about
the Caucasian female officer,

00:32:48.070 --> 00:32:50.200
who was walking up to my
car, what was in her mind?

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:51.630
So, I mean, just everything
you've mentioned,

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:54.120
I don't think we have
those conversations enough.

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:56.050
I don't know as much about
the law enforcement side

00:32:56.050 --> 00:32:58.460
but I feel like it's
something that has to be heard

00:32:58.460 --> 00:32:59.520
on both sides of the aisle.

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:02.610
That's definitely a live
conversation in a lot of ways.

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:05.870
So again, that could be a whole
nother topic of discussion.

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:09.260
But I think sometimes we don't
talk about what's happening

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:10.380
in the brains on both sides

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.140
and how once you put that together,

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:13.640
Amy I don't know if there's
anything you want to add

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:16.620
from your perspective
about those stressors

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:18.850
that come on both sides
of the aisle in that way.

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:23.850
- Oh yeah, (indistinct) empathy
is our way out, absolutely.

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:28.450
Now you can really understand
that when everybody's stressed

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:34.360
the worst things can happen
and forgiveness all around,

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:37.513
if you possibly can, how hard that is,

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:43.400
and then why deescalation
can be so helpful

00:33:43.507 --> 00:33:46.370
and that there are programs
and people out there

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:51.370
who are actively trying
to provide deescalation.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:56.760
Some of it based on neurobiology
to help people who are,

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:02.360
whose work constantly puts them

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:05.270
in these threatening situations.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:07.340
- Yeah, I think that's so important.

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:08.173
And clearly it doesn't ignore,

00:34:08.173 --> 00:34:10.650
it doesn't justify
anything that's happened

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:12.270
but I think it's a part
of the conversation

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:14.470
that we need to really
seriously talk about.

00:34:15.910 --> 00:34:17.493
- Constructive way forward.

00:34:18.460 --> 00:34:19.810
- Exactly.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:24.060
And one idea that's been lofted out there

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:29.060
is that maybe you don't
have law enforcement respond

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:32.824
to every emergency that comes up, right.

00:34:32.824 --> 00:34:37.824
If they're trained to respond
to violent criminal activity,

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:41.760
maybe they don't need to be called for,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:44.090
something that doesn't
fall under that scale

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:47.830
because you're already
hypervigilant, as you said.

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:50.200
So you're not thinking to deescalate,

00:34:50.200 --> 00:34:52.120
you're thinking, you're in
your fight or flight mode,

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:54.720
and it's just a speeding
ticket, you know what I mean?

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:56.075
When you're in fight or flight mode,

00:34:56.075 --> 00:34:59.220
so maybe there's some
restructuring around all of that.

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:03.100
I have a friend as well,
Nii, who's a police officer,

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:04.773
and he told me a story of him,

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:11.170
he's a black man, but he's chasing down

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:17.500
someone who has committed a violent crime.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:20.170
And it was like a domestic
abusive if I'm not mistaken.

00:35:20.170 --> 00:35:25.040
And he catches him and the guy
starts fighting him violently

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:26.823
he's on drugs, and,

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:30.287
he was in a situation where he was like,

00:35:30.287 --> 00:35:33.830
"Man, this guy is really dangerous."

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:37.893
And he had to make a
decision in that moment like,

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:43.730
as this guy broke through a
hold and took off running,

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:46.710
was it worth chasing after him

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:48.990
and getting into even more

00:35:48.990 --> 00:35:51.030
violent circumstance or situation?

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:52.647
And he had to process that.

00:35:52.647 --> 00:35:54.583
But the crazy part about it was,

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:59.110
after that call he's already,

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:02.020
his emotions and everything is heightened

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:03.590
and he's got to respond to another call

00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:06.770
that's nowhere near the
same type of circumstances.

00:36:06.770 --> 00:36:11.060
And how does he turn his
brain down, dial it down,

00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:12.610
to deal with what's in front of him

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:17.610
when he's just left a brawl,
with a violent individual.

00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:20.260
And so those are all the circumstances

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:23.080
that I don't think we're talking
about and we're processing.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:24.680
- Yeah, yeah that's so true.

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:25.513
And Amy, all the,

00:36:25.513 --> 00:36:26.817
I mean all the tools
you've been talking about

00:36:26.817 --> 00:36:29.560
and the processes in the brain
that we need to be aware of,

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:31.330
it seems like they're
all coming into play,

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:34.640
will need to be an essential
part of those conversations.

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:39.300
- Yeah, and are there things
like people who are specialized

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:44.300
in dealing with mental health emergencies

00:36:44.450 --> 00:36:47.633
who could be in that car
and there to help them.

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:53.160
Have pharmacological tools
to deal with somebody

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:58.160
who's really psychotic so that
things with physical force

00:37:02.600 --> 00:37:06.140
don't need to be this much, yeah.

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:08.590
Much more thoughtful
ways of how to proceed

00:37:08.590 --> 00:37:13.373
and protect the police as well
as the other people, yeah.

00:37:13.373 --> 00:37:15.170
- Yeah, yeah, this is so important.

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:16.920
I mean, to be honest I can add something,

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.277
we'll probably have a more
full extended conversation

00:37:19.277 --> 00:37:23.490
on this podcast at some point
'cause I think it's one that,

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:24.730
it's happening in small pockets,

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:27.250
but it really needs to be a much larger

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:29.420
and bigger conversation
in a lot of ways so-

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:30.700
- Yeah.

00:37:30.700 --> 00:37:32.510
- I was curious just as we talked about

00:37:32.510 --> 00:37:33.620
you both have music ties

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:36.380
but how do you see music
tying into this as well?

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:37.820
Amy you talked about the emotion.

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:39.830
How does the music tie in

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:41.550
both in terms of like
how we experience things

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:42.610
and in terms of the brain,

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:44.963
like what is the power that's there?

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:50.340
I'm putting you on the spot. (chuckles)

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:52.303
- I'll be very speculative.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:59.600
So in right-handed individuals,

00:37:59.600 --> 00:38:03.920
the left hemisphere is
usually what creates

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:08.140
and understands language
whereas the right hemisphere

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:12.270
creates and understands
emotion in language.

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:16.650
And to me, music is the right hemisphere

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:18.690
without necessarily the words.

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:22.530
And then of course we add words in songs.

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:26.160
But you don't even need those
to feel the emotion of songs.

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:28.633
So that's something I just wondered about.

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:34.940
It's thinking about music
as emotional expression

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:37.043
that doesn't even need words.

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:39.170
- That's well said.

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:44.950
- Yeah, I would not have been
able to say it like that,

00:38:44.950 --> 00:38:45.783
that's great.

00:38:50.440 --> 00:38:53.150
In my understanding in the
learning that I've had,

00:38:53.150 --> 00:38:54.660
one of the realizations that I've had is

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:59.440
that I've been writing songs
since I was a little kid

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:01.400
and probably

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:10.440
around 2000 and, I wanna say the iPhone,

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:14.593
the iPhone in terms of like
writing and taking notes

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:17.630
and computers and whatnot,

00:39:17.630 --> 00:39:20.460
I think that was more
revolutionary for me anyway

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:25.273
around 2011 and 2010 maybe.

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:31.490
And so I wonder this is just
me being speculative as well

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:35.040
but I wonder if all those
years that I spent time

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:39.460
actually using the motor
skills to write and to process

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:42.330
was helpful for my mental stability.

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:45.460
And as I began to switch
over and use the keyboard

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:47.350
and use my phone to write

00:39:47.350 --> 00:39:49.723
was I not getting all the same benefits?

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:51.840
There was something therapeutic

00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:54.380
about writing down those lyrics.

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:57.690
And also in light of what Amy just said,

00:39:57.690 --> 00:39:59.920
I love creative expression.

00:39:59.920 --> 00:40:01.410
I think it's very helpful for me.

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:05.440
I think it connects
with people, music does,

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:07.280
minor chords hit a certain type,

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:08.710
part of the mind in the brain,

00:40:08.710 --> 00:40:11.130
major chords, get you more excited.

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:16.080
But when I have to
actually take the creative

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:20.810
musical expression and then
add concepts and words,

00:40:20.810 --> 00:40:22.440
it becomes a little tougher.

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:26.101
It becomes a little more
like of a push uphill.

00:40:26.101 --> 00:40:28.390
So I'm wondering if
there's something to that

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:30.230
and you might have nailed something there.

00:40:30.230 --> 00:40:33.810
I know I need to have words
'cause it connects with people

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:35.550
but it's just very interesting

00:40:35.550 --> 00:40:39.390
that I can get a kick out
of just playing on the keys

00:40:39.390 --> 00:40:43.590
or hearing a jazz, saxophone solo,

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:45.963
and it still gives me that same emotion.

00:40:47.870 --> 00:40:49.710
- And it's especially an uphill push

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:52.274
if you had to make those words rhyme.

00:40:52.274 --> 00:40:54.063
(laughing loudly)

00:40:54.063 --> 00:40:55.156
- Tell me about it,

00:40:55.156 --> 00:40:56.202
(Nii laughing)

00:40:56.202 --> 00:40:57.604
tell me about it.

00:40:57.604 --> 00:40:59.420
Yeah I'm working on it.

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:00.760
- Wow, there's definitely something there.

00:41:00.760 --> 00:41:02.592
I mean, I'm just thinking
back, not to disclose

00:41:02.592 --> 00:41:06.180
too many Addy secrets,
my own song writing days

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:08.873
and just the nostalgia that
comes with it sometimes too.

00:41:08.873 --> 00:41:12.080
Like if I hear something I
wrote 20 years, 25 years ago,

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:14.472
the place that it takes me back to,

00:41:14.472 --> 00:41:16.560
(indistinct) that's not, I mean it's all,

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:19.390
the positive triggering
in a sense some ways

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:20.960
that's outside of my realm of expertise,

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:22.090
but you all are making me-

00:41:22.090 --> 00:41:24.720
- Now we need to hear
this music, I didn't even-

00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:26.446
(Nii and Amy laughing loudly)

00:41:26.446 --> 00:41:28.330
- I knew that was dangerous. (chuckles)

00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:29.940
- Don't worry, I won't
let you forget about it.

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:31.520
- I'm sure, we'll bring it out,

00:41:31.520 --> 00:41:34.400
we'll bring it out in some
shape or form. (chuckles)

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:37.176
- So, Nii, I also wrote
songs when I was a teenager.

00:41:37.176 --> 00:41:38.030
- Wow.

00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:40.382
- The two of us should
put out an album together.

00:41:40.382 --> 00:41:42.680
- Wow.

00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:46.500
- Okay, lemme find out that
it's got to happen now,

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:47.333
we need that,

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:50.370
who knows when two
neuroscientists get together

00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:53.260
what kind of songs are gonna
be created, we need that.

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:56.600
- Well, you gotta be pulled
into this mix too. (chuckles)

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:58.100
- All right, I'll see what I can do.

00:41:58.100 --> 00:42:00.550
I'll see if I have anything to contribute.

00:42:00.550 --> 00:42:02.550
The lyrics get a little too lofty for me.

00:42:02.550 --> 00:42:06.130
Get a little too academic for
me, I'll try to be the person

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:08.790
to make it make sense
to the common person.

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:10.480
- I was ready for you to
make a neuro-science comment

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:11.550
when you were starting to follow Amy

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:13.890
with that comment about
the music and your writing.

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:14.723
I was getting really excited

00:42:14.723 --> 00:42:16.310
about where you were gonna take it.

00:42:16.310 --> 00:42:19.480
- Well, listen, I don't,
I know the little bit

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:22.830
that I do know, and I'm
understanding the motor skills

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:25.340
and the parts of the brain that
are trying to work together

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:29.110
and so on and so forth,
and so again, like I said,

00:42:29.110 --> 00:42:33.373
I'm all about trying to find
as much healing as possible.

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:41.760
I'm honored to be able to be
in conversations like this

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:44.330
because I'm always wondering
like what are the new advances?

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:46.350
what's gonna happen, I mean,
look at what's happening?

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:50.010
We got my, I drive a car
that if I push a button,

00:42:50.010 --> 00:42:53.513
it can navigate itself
through city streets,

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:56.400
we gotta figure something
out with the brain.

00:42:56.400 --> 00:42:58.010
You know what I mean? (chuckles)

00:42:58.010 --> 00:42:58.907
- Yeah, it's important.

00:42:58.907 --> 00:43:00.690
And on a serious note too, I think it's,

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:04.310
I mean what you're doing
by pulling in those pieces

00:43:04.310 --> 00:43:06.280
and communicating that
to a large audience,

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:07.640
'cause that makes a difference too,

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:09.750
because I think that's part
of what has to happen here.

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:12.040
We need to have, and I think you were both

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:12.873
referring to that earlier.

00:43:12.873 --> 00:43:14.920
We need to make sure
that we communicate that

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:17.600
that is the brain that is
involved in these things

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:18.600
and not lose sight of that

00:43:18.600 --> 00:43:20.900
because that's where
some of the stigma comes,

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:22.310
that's where some of the fear comes

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:25.220
and all the concerns
about, if I broke my arm,

00:43:25.220 --> 00:43:26.110
I'm gonna take care of that

00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:28.090
and get it put in a cast so I can heal.

00:43:28.090 --> 00:43:31.010
But if it's my brain, "Oh,
I don't want to touch that."

00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:33.300
Just all of the reactions
that come with that.

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:35.678
So I think it's critical.

00:43:35.678 --> 00:43:40.330
- Yeah, I think that's such
an area for improvement.

00:43:40.330 --> 00:43:44.640
Reducing stigma for mental
health within medicine itself,

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:47.200
let alone within society,

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:51.330
and also of course to make
mental health resources

00:43:51.330 --> 00:43:56.258
so much more available and cheap.

00:43:56.258 --> 00:43:57.091
- Yeah.

00:43:58.090 --> 00:43:59.763
- Yeah, I totally agree.
- Yeah.

00:43:59.763 --> 00:44:02.403
- I think it's funny because to me,

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:05.520
if I think about like a megaphone,

00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:06.877
a megaphone is small on one end

00:44:06.877 --> 00:44:10.530
and it's bigger on the other
end, I feel like academia

00:44:10.530 --> 00:44:12.550
has the megaphone turn the opposite way.

00:44:12.550 --> 00:44:14.780
Like you got all this information

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:17.370
and then it's like, it
just gets kind of like-

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:18.203
- Yeah.

00:44:18.203 --> 00:44:22.408
- I gotta get this out
here to the general public.

00:44:22.408 --> 00:44:23.880
(chuckles)

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:25.000
- Yeah.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:26.430
- I love this type of thing,

00:44:26.430 --> 00:44:29.300
we're trying to figure out
how to get it out to people.

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:31.688
- Yeah, there's so much there.

00:44:31.688 --> 00:44:35.790
- So, on purpose I've
been making YouTube videos

00:44:35.790 --> 00:44:38.750
and posting them on
the Yale Medical School

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:43.750
YouTube channel to provide
this information to the public,

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:48.490
to doctors who are not in the same field,

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:52.413
about burnout, for example,
especially during COVID.

00:44:53.650 --> 00:44:58.650
So it is my own little way of
trying to get that information

00:44:58.870 --> 00:45:02.560
out there and Nii I sent
you one of the links

00:45:02.560 --> 00:45:04.850
that maybe you can put it on the website.

00:45:04.850 --> 00:45:06.470
- Yeah, I can definitely
put that up on the website.

00:45:06.470 --> 00:45:08.290
Great resource, yeah.

00:45:08.290 --> 00:45:09.730
- That's wonderful.

00:45:09.730 --> 00:45:11.160
- So I know we're running tight on time

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:12.678
but I did want to ask both of you,

00:45:12.678 --> 00:45:15.650
what legacy are you trying to leave

00:45:15.650 --> 00:45:17.800
in terms of your passion.

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:19.180
I mean, all the things
that we've talked about.

00:45:19.180 --> 00:45:22.020
Like how are you trying to
make sure that that gets out

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:25.410
and continues to make an
impact to those around you?

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:27.610
Amy I'll have you, you started
with the YouTube video,

00:45:27.610 --> 00:45:30.100
so I'll have you continue
to expand on that.

00:45:30.100 --> 00:45:35.100
- Well, I'm really hoping that
we continue to make progress

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:39.410
in creating medications
that really help us

00:45:39.410 --> 00:45:42.643
be our better selves, our better angels,

00:45:43.690 --> 00:45:46.910
so that when you feel stuck in loss,

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:49.150
there's something you can turn to

00:45:49.150 --> 00:45:51.970
that's really healthy.

00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:56.040
I also wanted to say
something I discovered

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:59.480
in preparing for today's discussion.

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:03.570
I was thinking a lot about
the power of gratitude

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:08.570
and forgiveness and so I looked and said,

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:13.220
I wonder if anybody has looked
at what happens in the brain

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:15.120
when you're feeling those emotions.

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:19.693
And I found a few studies
including one by the Damasio lab.

00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:25.530
And I was so struck that
when you're feeling gratitude

00:46:25.880 --> 00:46:30.880
or forgiveness, it
activates the same areas

00:46:31.590 --> 00:46:35.390
of prefrontal cortex that Rajita Sinha,

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:37.500
who was your guests last time,

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:39.490
has shown are the very circuits

00:46:39.490 --> 00:46:42.953
that help us overcome the stress response.

00:46:43.790 --> 00:46:48.790
So a real neural basis
for how those feelings

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:54.090
may really help us overcome
the toxic effects of stress.

00:46:54.890 --> 00:46:56.170
- That's so good.

00:46:56.170 --> 00:46:57.003
That's so good.

00:46:57.003 --> 00:46:59.250
I mean, it goes back
to what I think about,

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:01.890
what we're told in scripture a lot too,

00:47:01.890 --> 00:47:04.930
in terms of the importance
of serving others

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:08.420
in some ways it sounds to me
like the neurobiological basis

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:09.963
of what that is doing

00:47:09.963 --> 00:47:12.733
and just kind of ties
everything together, wow.

00:47:13.770 --> 00:47:14.680
- That's great.

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:19.680
- I felt so too, that it
relates up the song "Blessings"

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:25.400
and religion and science altogether

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:28.463
by activating the ventral
medial prefrontal cortex.

00:47:29.530 --> 00:47:30.970
- Wow. (chuckles)

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:33.614
You taught that the way only
you could tie that together.

00:47:33.614 --> 00:47:35.060
(Amy laughing)

00:47:35.060 --> 00:47:36.190
- Yeah, that's amazing.

00:47:36.190 --> 00:47:39.225
- And the day I see that in
the lyric, Lecrae will be-

00:47:39.225 --> 00:47:41.160
(indistinct)
(Nii laughing)

00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:43.833
- Listen, I don't know what to, I can,

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:48.830
that's interesting that she
says that because my legacy,

00:47:48.830 --> 00:47:52.010
I definitely feel like my
legacy is to figure out a way

00:47:54.350 --> 00:47:59.350
to process the information
that we currently have

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:04.433
and to shrink the amygdala so that it,

00:48:05.720 --> 00:48:07.770
there's gotta be a way
to shrink the amygdala

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:11.250
and to ignite the prefrontal cortex

00:48:11.250 --> 00:48:14.170
so that it can communicate
clearly to the amygdala

00:48:14.170 --> 00:48:17.300
during stress circumstances.

00:48:17.300 --> 00:48:20.210
And I think if I can just write a song

00:48:20.210 --> 00:48:21.660
that would allow that to happen

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:26.290
then the whole world is changed, clearly.

00:48:26.290 --> 00:48:29.310
That's my contribution and legacy.

00:48:29.310 --> 00:48:31.350
- I'm actually a little
bit scared by your insight.

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:32.790
'Cause there are studies that,

00:48:32.790 --> 00:48:33.877
maybe not shrinking the amygdala

00:48:33.877 --> 00:48:35.980
but they're looking at
those exact pathways.

00:48:35.980 --> 00:48:39.180
So somehow you tapped
into something. (chuckles)

00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:41.060
- Oh, man, oh,

00:48:41.060 --> 00:48:45.400
no, but sincerely I
see myself as a bridge.

00:48:45.400 --> 00:48:48.990
I live in a lot of different communities,

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:50.953
obviously the faith-based community,

00:48:51.940 --> 00:48:56.320
the African-American community,
the musical community.

00:48:56.320 --> 00:49:01.320
And as someone who has
struggled with mental illness,

00:49:02.990 --> 00:49:07.430
I've seen the benefit of
the scientific community

00:49:07.430 --> 00:49:10.150
being able to speak to these places.

00:49:10.150 --> 00:49:11.930
And I wanna be a bridge.

00:49:11.930 --> 00:49:14.383
I think that we've seen,

00:49:16.300 --> 00:49:17.860
that's not the only
legacy that want to live

00:49:17.860 --> 00:49:20.610
but as specifically as
it pertains to this, yes.

00:49:20.610 --> 00:49:23.920
We've seen obviously
within the black community,

00:49:23.920 --> 00:49:28.920
just like the total, like, I
don't know what therapy is,

00:49:29.457 --> 00:49:34.290
I don't do all that stuff
and just them understanding

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:36.500
the necessity of a lot of these things.

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:38.694
In the musical community, a
lot of artists and musicians

00:49:38.694 --> 00:49:43.560
are brilliant and struggling
with mental illness

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:45.390
simultaneously, and
they don't even realize

00:49:45.390 --> 00:49:50.390
that a lot of their art
is stemmed from depression

00:49:50.860 --> 00:49:54.200
or anxiety or other types of disorders.

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:57.160
And so just helping them
understand that relationship

00:49:57.160 --> 00:49:58.500
so that they can find health.

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:01.020
I don't think it's coincidental that,

00:50:01.020 --> 00:50:03.970
the musician community has
its quote unquote 27 Club

00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:07.193
where all these artists
died at 27 years old.

00:50:08.600 --> 00:50:13.080
And so, and then obviously
in the faith-based community

00:50:13.080 --> 00:50:15.160
to erase a lot of the stigmas as well.

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:17.730
I think there's this misconception

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:22.730
that the brain is not a physical entity,

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:26.760
that it is all spiritual

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:30.720
and so to take medication is unspiritual,

00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:35.720
to seek therapy outside of a
pastor or a priest or whatnot

00:50:36.490 --> 00:50:38.890
is somehow unbiblical.

00:50:38.890 --> 00:50:43.890
And so just to close those
gaps would be very encouraging

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:48.660
for me as something that
I'd strive to be able to do.

00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:51.740
And so, yeah, I would
love to see that happen.

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:54.050
And I guess what I would say is this

00:50:54.050 --> 00:50:59.050
is that I think that we're all in a band

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:02.370
and too many of us are playing solos,

00:51:02.370 --> 00:51:05.248
and we sound a lot better
when we play together.

00:51:05.248 --> 00:51:08.570
When all this stuff is mixed together

00:51:08.570 --> 00:51:12.960
and now there's information
that is widespread.

00:51:14.770 --> 00:51:19.610
And so you can embrace some
of these cultural things,

00:51:19.610 --> 00:51:22.770
but then also add the
scientific elements to it

00:51:22.770 --> 00:51:24.754
and we're all healthier.

00:51:24.754 --> 00:51:26.655
- Yeah, yeah.

00:51:26.655 --> 00:51:28.710
Well, I appreciate both
of you for doing that

00:51:28.710 --> 00:51:32.160
